---
/**
 * LegalPage — a whole legal page  (/privacy-policy, /terms-of-service)
 * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * Composes the four things a legal page is made of: the page shell, the compact
 * document header, the clause run, and the site's closing band. It takes the
 * finished page object and its route, and nothing else — so the two routes are
 * three lines each and cannot drift apart from one another.
 *
 * WHY THIS OWNS THE LAYOUT
 *   It is a routed page component, which on this site means it owns its
 *   <BaseLayout> and its closing band — the shape `IndustryDetailPage` and
 *   `ServiceDetailPage` already have. That is also what lets a route choose
 *   between a finished page and a reservation shell in ONE expression
 *   (`{page ? <LegalPage … /> : <ReservationPage … />}`) instead of splitting
 *   the branch inside a layout, which is how `/services/[slug]` and
 *   `/industries/[slug]` do it.
 *
 * WHY IT TAKES THE WHOLE PAGE OBJECT
 *   `page` is non-null here by construction: the route only reaches this
 *   component when `legal-privacy.js` / `legal-terms.js` has a real object. An
 *   explicit prop interface would mean five props threaded through two routes
 *   for no gain, and the object is already the single source of the copy.
 *   Nothing here reads a clause from anywhere else.
 *
 * WHY THE CLAUSE BODY IS AN ORDERED LIST OF BLOCKS
 *   The copy interleaves its parts and the order carries meaning: §1 and §2 of
 *   the privacy policy each introduce a list ("…such as:") and then say one more
 *   thing after it, and two clauses end with an action link. A `paragraphs: []`
 *   plus an `items: []` shape cannot express "a paragraph after the list" — it
 *   can only ever render one order — so the body is an array of blocks and is
 *   rendered in the order the data gives. A block is one line each:
 *
 *     { p: '…' }                      a paragraph
 *     { list: ['…', '…'] }            a bulleted list
 *     { link: { label, href } }       a standalone action link (ArrowLink)
 *     { entity: true }                the business name line, from `site.name`
 *     { mail: true }                  the contact email line, from `site.email`
 *
 *   `entity` and `mail` are derived rather than typed on purpose. Both documents
 *   print the company name and the contact address, and a legal page that states
 *   a contact address different from the one in the footer is a real defect —
 *   so neither string exists in the data at all and neither can drift.
 *
 *   The email line carries its own class (`.lg-section__contact`) rather than the
 *   paragraph class, even though it is a paragraph and shares its styling. That
 *   keeps `.lg-section__para` meaning exactly one thing — "one paragraph the data
 *   declared" — which is what lets a gate compare the rendered paragraph count
 *   against the data's. With both classes on it, the derived line counted as a
 *   clause paragraph and the count was off by one on every clause that carries
 *   one, on both documents.
 *
 * WHY THE CLAUSES ARE AN <ol> WITH NO MARKERS
 *   A legal document is an ordered sequence by construction ("see section 4" is
 *   a normal sentence on a page like this), so the run is an <ol> and each clause
 *   its <li>, which is what lets assistive technology announce "list item 4 of
 *   10". The list marker itself is suppressed because the clause NUMBER is
 *   already part of each heading — the brief writes its headings as
 *   "4. Reference Files and Product Information" — and printing a marker as well
 *   would number every clause twice.
 *
 *   That is also why the placeholder-era device's CSS counter is gone. While the
 *   copy did not exist the headings had no numbers, so `legal.css` generated
 *   01, 02, … into a reserved left column. The real headings carry their own
 *   numbering, so keeping the counter would have printed "01  1. Information We
 *   Collect". The number the reader sees is now the one in the heading, and it
 *   is the one a gate can compare against the brief verbatim.
 *
 * WHY THE CLAUSE RUN HAS NO ACCESSIBLE NAME
 *   The header band is labelled by the H1 it contains. The clause run is that
 *   document's text, so labelling it too would put two landmarks on the page
 *   with the same name — worse for a screen-reader user than the unnamed section
 *   the reservation pages already ship (`.reserved-body` carries no
 *   `aria-labelledby` either). The document outline is carried by the <h2>s
 *   inside it.
 *
 * WHY THE HEADER CARRIES A "LAST UPDATED" LINE
 *   The brief requires one ("Last updated: September 2026") and requires it to
 *   be the real current month, not an invented earlier date. It is data, not a
 *   build timestamp: a date derived from the build would silently re-date the
 *   policy every time the site is rebuilt, which is the one thing a "last
 *   updated" line must not do. See `legal-privacy.js` for where it is declared
 *   once for both documents.
 *
 * ONE CLOSING ACTION, NOT TWO
 *   `<FinalCTA>` emits exactly one control by design (site spec §19 forbids
 *   competing buttons in the closing band), so nothing here adds a second.
 *
 * IMPORT ORDER IS LOAD-BEARING
 *   Layout first, then this page's sheet, or the rules below lose every
 *   same-specificity conflict against the shared core. `npm run audit` fails the
 *   build if that regresses, and its "shared device" rule also fails the build
 *   if a page renders `.lg-*` without this sheet — which is the defect that
 *   shipped on `/faq` when it rendered a shared device from a sheet it had not
 *   imported.
 *
 * Styles live in `legal.css` (`.lg-hero*`, `.lg-sections*`, `.lg-section*`).
 * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
 */
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import '../../styles/legal.css';

import ArrowLink from '../ArrowLink.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../Breadcrumbs.astro';
import FinalCTA from '../FinalCTA.astro';

import { breadcrumbItems, breadcrumbSchema, webPageSchema } from '../../data/schema.js';
import { site } from '../../data/site.js';

/**
 * One line of a clause's body. Declared as optional keys rather than a
 * discriminated union so the template can test them in order without the
 * narrowing gymnastics a union would need — the same looseness the rest of this
 * codebase's prop interfaces use.
 */
type Block = {
  p?: string;
  list?: string[];
  link?: { label: string; href: string };
  entity?: boolean;
  mail?: boolean;
};

interface Props {
  /** Root-relative route, used for canonical + og:url. */
  path: string;
  /**
   * The finished document. Every string on the page comes from here except the
   * two that are derived from `site.js` — see `entity` / `mail` above and
   * `src/data/legal-page.js` for the shape.
   */
  page: {
    meta: { title: string; description: string };
    breadcrumbs: Array<{ label: string; href?: string }>;
    hero: {
      eyebrow: string;
      title: string;
      description: string;
      /** "Last updated: …" — a fixed editorial value, never the build date. */
      updated: string;
    };
    sections: Array<{
      /** Stable, unique within this document, URL-safe address for the clause. */
      anchor: string;
      /** Carries its own number, exactly as the brief writes it ("4. Data Security"). */
      heading: string;
      /** Rendered in the order given. At least one block. */
      body: Block[];
    }>;
    finalCta: {
      eyebrow: string;
      title: string;
      description: string;
      cta: { label: string; href: string };
    };
  };
  /** Unique per page: the header band is labelled by this id. */
  titleId?: string;
}

const { path, page, titleId = 'lg-title' } = Astro.props;
const { meta, breadcrumbs, hero, sections, finalCta } = page;

const schema = [
  webPageSchema({ title: meta.title, description: meta.description, path }),
  breadcrumbSchema(breadcrumbItems(breadcrumbs)),
];
---

<BaseLayout title={meta.title} description={meta.description} path={path} schema={schema}>
  <section class="lg-hero" aria-labelledby={titleId}>
    {/* `container--narrow`, NOT the wide `.container` the other page heroes
        use. A legal document is one column, so the header and the clause run
        have to share a left edge: with the wide container the H1 sat at 112px
        while the clause run sat at 322px — a 210px offset that is CONSTANT at
        every desktop width, because the wide container stops growing at
        1280px. See the note in `legal.css`. */}
    <div class="container container--narrow">
      <Breadcrumbs items={breadcrumbs} class="lg-hero__breadcrumbs" />

      <div class="lg-hero__head">
        <p class="eyebrow">{hero.eyebrow}</p>
        <h1 class="lg-hero__title" id={titleId}>{hero.title}</h1>
        <p class="lg-hero__lead">{hero.description}</p>
        <p class="lg-hero__updated">{hero.updated}</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--white">
    <div class="container container--narrow">
      <ol class="lg-sections">
        {
          sections.map((section) => (
            <li class="lg-section" id={section.anchor}>
              <h2 class="lg-section__title">{section.heading}</h2>

              <div class="lg-section__body">
                {section.body.map((block) =>
                  block.list ? (
                    <ul class="lg-section__list">
                      {block.list.map((item) => (
                        <li class="lg-section__item">{item}</li>
                      ))}
                    </ul>
                  ) : block.link ? (
                    <ArrowLink
                      class="lg-section__link"
                      label={block.link.label}
                      href={block.link.href}
                    />
                  ) : block.entity ? (
                    <p class="lg-section__entity">{site.name}</p>
                  ) : block.mail ? (
                    <p class="lg-section__contact">
                      Email:{' '}
                      <a class="lg-section__mail" href={`mailto:${site.email}`}>
                        {site.email}
                      </a>
                    </p>
                  ) : (
                    <p class="lg-section__para">{block.p}</p>
                  )
                )}
              </div>
            </li>
          ))
        }
      </ol>
    </div>
  </section>

  <FinalCTA
    eyebrow={finalCta.eyebrow}
    title={finalCta.title}
    description={finalCta.description}
    cta={finalCta.cta}
  />
</BaseLayout>
